{
  "evidence": "A: Mandatory National Service; legal annual migration cap that falls each year; monthly Rwanda removal flights; defence spending to 2.5% by 2030; 8,000 more neighbourhood police and tougher sentences; halve employee NI and abolish main rate NI for the self‑employed; no rises in income tax or VAT; welfare reform with tougher sanctions and a new Fraud Bill; NHS funding up each year with more nurses and doctors; protect the Union and British values; implement the Cass Review, protect single‑sex spaces, and define sex in law as biological. B: Unshakeable NATO commitment and nuclear deterrent; Border Security Command and tougher counter‑espionage; Neighbourhood Policing Guarantee and new offences; strict fiscal rules with no rises in NI/Income Tax/VAT; cap corporation tax at 25% with permanent full expensing; New Deal for Working People (ban exploitative zero‑hours, end fire‑and‑rehire, day‑one rights); major NHS and social care expansion; clean energy superpower plan and environmental protections.",
  "reasoning": "Authority is the electorate’s top value, and A puts greater weight on authority/loyalty themes (national service, stricter borders, defence, policing, Union/values) while strongly signalling proportionality (tax cuts, welfare sanctions, anti‑fraud) and adequate care via NHS investment. B offers some authority and proportionality but leans more on equality and worker‑protection planks, which are lower‑priority in this profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "A"
}
